ugh points in columns 3-6 with slopes that are rational numbers not equal to 0, -1, infinite. Since we have 4 lines, we can try to assign each line to cover a \"diagonal\" of the triangle: e.g., line through (3,1) and (6,1) is horizontal, not allowed. So we need to avoid horizontal. Maybe we can use lines that go through (3,1) and (5,2) slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2, sunny. That line also passes through (4,1.5) not integer. So line L1: (3,1) and (5,2). Good.\n\nLine L2: (3,2) and (6,1) slope = (1-2)/(6-3) = -1/3, sunny. That line also passes through (4,5/3) not integer, (5,4/3) not integer. So L2: (3,2) and (6,1). Good.\n\nLine L3: (3,3) and (4,1) slope = (1-3)/(4-3) = -2, sunny. That line also passes through (5,-1) not in T_6. So L3: (3,3) and (4,1). Good.\n\nLine L4: (3,4) and (5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-3) = -3/2, sunny. That line also passes through (4,2.5) not integer. So L4: (3,4) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points:\n\n- L1: (3,1),(5,2)\n- L2: (3,2),(6,1)\n- L3: (3,3),(4,1)\n- L4: (3,4),(5,1)\n\nRemaining points: column 4: (4,2),(4,3) not covered; column 5: (5,1) covered, (5,2) covered; column 6: (6,1) covered. Column 3: all points covered (3,1)-(3,4). So we still need to cover (4,2) and (4,3). We have no more sunny lines. Could we adjust lines to include these points? Perhaps we can assign some lines to have three points each, covering more columns. For example, line L1 could also include (4,? ) maybe (4,? ) such that collinearity holds. Let's see if we can find a line that passes through (3,1), (4,2), (5,3) but (5,3) not in T_6 (since (5,3) sum=8 >7). So can't have three points. Maybe line through (3,1), (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, sunny. That line also passes through (5,3) not allowed. So line L1 could be (3,1),(4,2). Then we need to cover (5,2) elsewhere. Let's try to restructure.\n\nGoal: cover all points in columns 3-6 with 4 lines. Let's try to assign each line to cover exactly one point in each column (so each line has 4 points). That would be id