n-integer y, which is fine because we don't need to cover any point in column 4 with that line. So we can have a sunny line through (5,1) with slope, say, 1/2 (or any rational not 0,-1). That line will intersect column 4 at y = 1 - 1/2 = 0.5, not a point we need to cover. So (5,1) can be covered by a sunny line that only covers points in columns 5,3,2 maybe. Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover all points in columns 2-5. Let's try to construct 4 sunny lines:\n\n- L1: line through (5,1) and (3,2) maybe? Let's compute slope: (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, which is sunny (not 0,∞,-1). Does it intersect column 4 at integer y? At x=4, y = 1 + (-1/2)*(4-5) = 1 + (-1/2)*(-1) = 1 + 1/2 = 1.5, not integer. So L1 covers (5,1) and (3,2). It also passes through column 2 at x=2: y = 1 + (-1/2)*(2-5) = 1 + (-1/2)*(-3) = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L1 covers only those two points.\n\n- L2: line through (4,1) and (2,2)? Slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, same slope as L1. But that line would be parallel to L1, but not same line because they have different intercepts. Let's compute line through (4,1) and (2,2): slope -1/2, equation: y-1 = -1/2 (x-4). At x=5, y = 1 -1/2*(5-4) = 1 - 1/2 = 0.5, not integer. At x=3, y = 1 -1/2*(3-4) = 1 + 1/2 = 1.5, not integer. So L2 covers (4,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- L3: line through (4,2) and (2,3)? Slope = (3-2)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -1/2 again. So L3: y-2 = -1/2 (x-4). At x=5, y = 2 -1/2*(5-4) = 2 - 1/2 = 1.5, not integer. At x=3, y = 2 -1/2*(3-4) = 2 + 1/2 = 2.5, not integer. So L3 covers (4,2) and (2,3). Good.\n\n- L4: line through (3,1) and (2,4)? Slope = (4-1)/(2-3) = 3/(-1) = -3, which is sunny (not 0,∞,-1). At x=5, y = 1 + (-3)*(5-3) = 1 - 6 = -5, not needed. At x=4, y = 1 + (-3)*(4-3) = 1 - 3 = -2, not needed. So L4 covers (3,1) and (2,4). But we still have uncovered points: (3,3) and (2,1). Actually we have covered:\n\n- L1: (5,1), (3,2)\n- L2: (4,1), (2,2)\n- L3: (4,2), (2,3)\n- L4: (3,1), (2,4)\n\nRemaining points: (3,3) and (2,1). Also (3,? maybe (3,3) is uncovered, (2,1) uncovered. Also (3,? maybe (3,? we have (3,1) covered, (3,2) covered, (3,3) uncovered). Column 2 points: (2,1) uncovered, (2,2) covered, (2,3) covered, (2,4) covered. So we have two uncovered points left. We have used all 4 sunny lines. So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that covers three points across columns, not just two. For example, line through (5,1), (3,3), (2,5?) but column 2 only goes up to y=4. So maybe line through (5,1), (3,3) slope = (3-1)/(3-5) = 2/(-2) = -1, which is not allowed. So can't.\n\nMaybe line through (5,1), (3,1) slope = (1-1)/(3-5) = 0, not allowed.\n\nMaybe line through (5,1), (2,4) slope = (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/(-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus (5,1) cannot be paired with any point in column 2 or 3 or 4 that yields a sunny slope? Let's check slopes:\n\n- (5,1) with (4,1): slope 0 (bad)\n- (5,1) with (4,2): slope -1 (bad)\n- (5,1) with (3,1): slope 0 (bad)\n- (5,1) with (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -1/2 (good)\n- (5,1) with (3,3): slope (3-1)/(3-5) = 2/(-2) = -1 (bad)\n- (5,1) with (2,1): slope (1-1)/(2-5) = 0 (bad)\n- (5,1) with (2,2): slope (2-1)/(2-5) = 1/(-3) = -1/3 (good)\n- (5,1) with (2,3): slope (3-1)/(2-5) = 2/(-3) = -2/3 (good)\n- (5,1) with (2,4): slope (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/(-3) = -1 (bad)\n\nSo (5,1) can be paired with (3,2), (2,2), (2,3) with sunny slopes. So we can try to cover (5,1) with (2,2) maybe, and then (2,2) also appears in column 2. But we need to cover all points in column 2. Let's try to design a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can think of a bipartite matching: each line picks at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point to a line such that each line picks at most one per column. This is like a Latin rectangle: we need to fill a 4x4 grid (columns 2-5) with 4 lines, each line picks at most one point per column, and each point must be assigned to some line. This is possible if we can find a set of 4 lines that each pass through exactly one point in each column (maybe some lines skip some columns). But we need to cover all points, not just one per column. Since there are many points per column, each line can cover at most one per column, so we need at least as many lines as the maximum column size, which is k (size of column n-k+1). For k=4, the largest column size is 4 (column 2). So we need at least 4 lines, which we have. So it's plausible.\n\nNow we need to find 4 sunny lines that each cover exactly one point in column 2, and collectively cover all points in column 2. So each of the 4 points in column 2 must be assigned to a distinct line. Similarly, each line must also cover a point in each of the other columns (maybe not all). But we need to ensure that the points assigned to a line across columns are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nThus we need to find a set of 4 lines L1,...,L4 such that:\n\n- L_i passes through (2, i) for i=1,2,3,4 (i.e., each line covers a distinct point in column 2).\n- For each column c = 3,4,5, each line passes through some point (c, y_c_i) (maybe different for each line) such that the points are collinear.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines each passing through a distinct point in column 2 and also passing through some point in column 3,4,5 (maybe not all). This is a system of linear equations.\n\nLet's try to find lines of the form y = m_i x + b_i that pass through (2, i). Then we need to see if we can choose m_i and b_i such that for each column c, the y-coordinate at x=c is integer and within the allowed range (1 ≤ y ≤ n+1-c). Also slope m_i must not be 0, ∞, -1.\n\nWe can try to find lines with slopes that are rational numbers with denominator maybe 1 or 2, etc.\n\nLet's attempt to find lines that pass through (2,1) and (5,1) is horizontal (bad). So (2,1) cannot be paired with (5,1). It can be paired with (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 (good). So line through (2,1) and (4,2) has slope 1/2, equation: y - 1 = (1/2)(x - 2). At x=5, y = 1 + (1/2)*(5-2) = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. At x=3, y = 1 + (1/2)*(3-2) = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5, not integer. So this line covers (2,1) and (4,2) only.\n\n(2,2) can be paired with (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 (good). So line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope -1/3, equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 2). At x=4, y = 2 - 1/3*(4-2) = 2 - 2/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333, not integer. At x=3, y = 2 - 1/3*(3-2) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. So line covers (2,2) and (5,1) only.\n\n(2,3) can be paired with (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 (good). Equation: y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 2). At x=4, y = 3 - 2/3*(4-2) = 3 - 4/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. At x=3, y = 3 - 2/3*(3-2) = 3 - 2/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So line covers (2,3) and (5,1) only.\n\n(2,4) can be paired with (5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1 (bad"
